  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms this\nafternoon and Wednesday. There is a risk for strong to severe thunderstorms,\nas well as a marginal risk for localized heavy rainfall leading to\nflash flooding.\n\nA surface cold front will gradually sag southward across the northern\ntier of the region this afternoon into evening before stalling around\nthe I-80 or I-78 corridors come Wednesday. During this time, a few\nupper level shortwave pulses will rendezvous with the surface cold\nfront, bringing rounds of showers and storms through Wednesday morning.\nThe first round of storms this afternoon and evening will be across\nthe northern areas near the Poconos and possibly into the Lehigh\nValley. Other more scattered activity is possible across central\nNJ. The upper vort maxs are difficult to track and the CAMs guidance\nis very inconsistent, so we'll continue with blended guidance as\nwell as satellite/radar trends for our updates. That said, instability\nacross the region does look to be limited to the 1000-2000 J/kg\nrange with limited deep layer shear, which will, therefore, limit\nthe severe weather potential today. SPC has expanded the Marginal\nRisk (level 1 out of 5) farther northeast now covering our\nentire CWA. Also the entire area remains in the Marginal Risk \n(level 1 out of 4) for excessive rainfall.\n\nThe latest guidance now suggests a second round of showers and \nperhaps some convective thunderstorms to arrive early Wednesday \nmorning with the potential for heavy downpours during the Wednesday\nmorning rush hour. While there remains a fair amount of uncertainty\nwith this setup it appears that this round may be a result of a\ndecaying upstream MCS from the Ohio Valley. This setup remains \nhighly uncertain and fully dependent on how the volatile setup \nacross the Ohio Valley eventually pans out through the overnight\nhours. Areas that are favored for higher pops and heavy rains \nwill be Delmarva and south NJ, possibly into Philadelphia. \n\nAdditional rounds of upper level shortwaves look to ride along the\nquasi-stationary boundary which will support rounds of convection\ncontinuing at least through Wednesday night, and possibly into \nThursday. The front looks to push through by late Thursday, and \na much drier and cooler airmass will arrive by Friday and \nSaturday as a result. This will bring us a more pronounced break\nin convective potential and more pleasant weather conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions will persist into the \nmiddle of the week, but humidity will not be as oppressive as \nrecent days.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern will remain in place across\nour region. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be in place\nthrough the middle of the week as Bermuda high pressure persists\noffshore. This will keep a very warm and moist airmass in place\nacross the region through the period.\n\nHot and humid conditions will continue into the middle of the\nweek, although slightly lower temperatures and dewpoints will\narrive for Wednesday. Dewpoints are anticipated to mix out into\nthe mid to upper 60s again this afternoon. This will give some \nslight reprieve from the excessive humidity we've experienced in\nrecent days, though highs will still be in the upper 80s to mid\n90s. The lower dewpoints will result in maximum heat indices \nmainly in the mid to upper 90s, though could get near 100 \ndegrees in Delmarva and near the coast in New Jersey. Therefore,\nhot and humid, but not to the point where any heat headlines are\nneeded.\n\nBy Friday and Saturday with the front finally having pushed through,\na much drier and more cooler airmass will arrive, which will bring\nmuch more pleasant weather conditions. Dewpoints may drop into the\n50s again for many areas with highs forecast to be in the low to\nmid 80s. Dewpoints look to start rebounding into the 60s again \nby Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is potential for minor tidal flooding \nduring the evening high tides this week along the Atlantic \ncoast, Delaware Bay, and tidal Delaware River, especially on \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAs we approach the New Moon tomorrow, astronomical tides for \nthe evening high tide cycles will increase to around a half foot\nbelow minor flood stage in many areas this week along the \nAtlantic coasts of New Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and the\ntidal Delaware River. With tide departures running around a \nhalf foot above average, this will raise a threat of minor flood\nstage being reached for these areas.\n\nWe will continue to monitor the tide departures in the coming\ntotal water level forecasts. Coastal Flood Advisories have been\nissued for areas along the Delaware Bay for this evening's high\ntide along with a new set of Coastal Flood Advisories for\ntomorrow evening's high tide cycle. At least spotty minor tidal\nflooding should be anticipated around the times of the evening \nhigh tides this week with the potential for additional\nAdvisories depending on the tidal departures. The highest \nastronomical tides will o",
